1.22.2: the D610 verified against LibRaw - image area, matrix, side choice

A real D610 NEF (14-bit Nikon compressed, 6080x4028) settled three
things 1.22.0 had guessed at.

The image area. The D610's CropHiSpeed tag declares no crop
("6080x4028 cropped to 6080x4028 at 0,0"), so bodies that declare
nothing now get their image area from a small table keyed by model
and raw size, a mismatch meaning no crop. The D600/D610 entry is
measured on the file: 46 masked columns at the right (8 black
reference columns, 6 junk, 32 dummies at 1) and no masked rows, so
6034x4028, as LibRaw delivers; the other entries (D3200/D3300, D7000,
D800, D90, D750) are the cameras' JPEG sizes from memory, which may
trim a few picture columns and never show masked ones. Only the side
of the masked band is read from the data, by the band's darkest line:
a masked band always holds lines at the black level, a picture edge
does not. The band's mean was tried first and misled on this very
file (the junk columns lift it above a dark wall at the picture's
left edge). A generic "find the black border" detector was tried and
dropped too: it cropped a legitimately black edge column.

The color matrix. The D610 entry in the NEF table was wrong (another
body's numbers); it now carries the dcraw/LibRaw coefficients
{8178,-2245,-609,-4857,12394,2776,-1207,2086,7298}. The other seven
entries were checked against LibRaw's table and were right.

The verification. Decoded scene-linear sRGB from polajuice against
LibRaw's (camera WB, bilinear demosaic, no auto-bright, linear
output) over the whole 24 MP frame: mean absolute difference 1.7e-5,
99.9th percentile 5e-5, at LibRaw's 16-bit quantization; the few
larger pixels are clipped highlights the two clip differently. The
unverified flag on the NEF door comes off.

Tests: the table lookup and the side choice on a 40x30 readout
masked on either side; the bordered synthetic raws of 1.22.0 still
decode to the bit as the plain files.

